            <title><![CDATA[The Birds of GTA 6: A Field Guide to Vice City and Leonida's Wildlife (342 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://www.audubon.org/magazine/birds-of-gta-6-field-guide-vice-city-wildlife</link>
            <guid>50602843</guid>
            <pubDate>Sun, 22 Nov 2026 19:10:34 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://www.audubon.org/magazine/birds-of-gta-6-field-guide-vice-city-wildlife">https://www.audubon.org/magazine/birds-of-gta-6-field-guide-vice-city-wildlife</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50602843">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div data-component-id="nas_layout:nas-article-3col-template" id="main" role="main">
                          <div data-component-id="nas_blocks:universal-hero" data-hero-color="dominant" data-dom-color-wide="96, 85, 67" data-dom-color-tall="96, 85, 67">
              <div>
          
          

          <p>From Roseate Spoonbills in the Grassrivers to gulls over Vice Beach, here's what it takes to find Leonida’s birds, how the spotting mechanics work, and what Rockstar’s artists got right.</p>
          
          
          
        </div>
            
              <figure data-component-id="nas_blocks:media-content">
  
  <figcaption data-component-id="nas_patterns:media-attribution">
  <span>Still: Rockstar Games</span>
</figcaption>

</figure>

          </div>


        <section property="schema:text" data-component-id="nas_blocks:field--nas-body"> <!--StartFragment--><p><span>I finished collecting the bricks of cocaine and landed my bullet-ridden seaplane at the dock. It was time for birdwatching.<o:p></o:p></span></p><p><span>A few minutes after midnight on Thursday morning, I joined millions of other gamers for the release of </span><em><span>Grand Theft Auto VI</span></em><span>, the latest in the smash video game series from Rockstar Games. The new installment is already a worldwide sensation, earning more than </span><a href="https://www.forbes.com/sites/conormurray/2026/11/19/grand-theft-auto-vi-passes-18-billion-in-revenue-on-release-day-eclipses-the-odysseys-box-office-total/?streamIndex=0"><span>$1.8 billion in revenue</span></a><span> the day it was released.&nbsp;<o:p></o:p></span></p><p><em><span>Grand Theft Auto</span></em><span>, or </span><em><span>GTA</span></em><span>, games are famous for their “open worlds,” which allow players to explore massive, intricately detailed versions of America. Much of that exploration is focused on mayhem and chaos: The story missions usher players through criminal underworlds, and players are at any time free to steal cars, rob stores and pedestrians, and initiate anarchy.&nbsp;<o:p></o:p></span></p><p><span>But </span><em><span>GTA 6</span></em><span> has more going on than just criminality—it’s also a world filled with wildlife. Previous </span><em><span>GTA</span></em><span> games were set in fictionalized versions of New York City and Hollywood, but the new one returns to Vice City, its version of Miami, which was also the setting for 2002’s installment. Unlike that game, however, </span><em><span>GTA 6</span></em><span>’s South Florida is complete with dozens of species of birds, reptiles, amphibians, fish, mammals, and other creatures. The in-game Progress menu tracks all kinds of items to find, including 338 vehicles, 205 weapons, and, of primary interest to me, at least 60 bird species.&nbsp;</span></p><p><span>I made it my mission to find them all as quickly as possible.</span></p><p>(Side note: Pre-release marketing for <em>GTA</em> 6 touted 62 bird species, but the official birds Progress screen only lists 60, with a handful of others listed on the hunting screen.)<span><o:p></o:p><o:p></o:p></span></p><!--EndFragment--><blockquote><p lang="en" dir="ltr">I am one hour in and I’ve found 16/60 birds https://t.co/81x42O1BuY</p><p>— The Birdist (@TheBirdist) <a href="https://x.com/TheBirdist/status/2123300117705105793?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw">November 19, 2026</a></p></blockquote><!--StartFragment--><p><span>You begin the game as Jason, a low-level criminal working to raise money to support his young family. You’re equipped with zoom binoculars before the very first mission—tracking drug runners through a swamp and escaping with the seaplane—and I used them immediately, stopping mid-lurk to scan some Double-crested Cormorants on a dock. (Distracted from the job at hand to look at birds—just like real life!) They looked like Double-crested Cormorants!&nbsp;</span></p><p><span>After the first mission the player is free to explore the entirety of the map, stealing cars, robbing stores, or just birding wherever they want. The game makes birding easy: Looking at any bird through the in-game binoculars gives players the option to hold a button to “study” it, thereby adding it to the Progress menu. Simply standing on the back deck of Jason’s first safehouse yields a bunch of species like Brown Pelican and Laughing Gull. It’s just like being in South Florida!</span></p><div data-component-id="nas_bean:bean-block-green-blockquote">
          <p> I found a few more species right in Jason’s backyard, including American Crow, House Sparrow, and South Florida-appropriate Monk Parakeets. </p>
              
      </div>
<p><span>Looking south from the back deck, I spotted another bird perched on an adjacent dock, with a few people gathered at the end. Looking through my binoculars </span><a href="https://x.com/TheBirdist/status/2123394798996558131?a=enlj6KQ8F4qw-PKwBLg&amp;s=20"><span>I spotted my first Herring Gull</span></a><span>—and noticed that the people, a woman and three men, were fully and visibly naked. Jason apparently lives next to a nudist resort. It’s just like being in South Florida!</span></p><p><span>Two down, 58 to go. I found a few more species right in Jason’s backyard, including American Crow, House Sparrow, and South Florida-appropriate Monk Parakeets, before venturing out into the huge map. My strategy for finding birds in </span><em><span>GTA 6</span></em><span> was the same as finding birds in real life: look in as many habitats as I could find. I started by “borrowing” a fanboat off a nearby dock and heading back to the swampy areas near the seaplane mission. There were tons of birds across the open water and in the marsh: Caspian Terns, Mottled Ducks, Sanderlings, Spotted Sandpipers, Great Blue Herons, Glossy and White Ibis, and … flocks of Whooping Cranes?<o:p></o:p></span></p><!--EndFragment--><blockquote><p lang="en" dir="ltr">incredibly healthy populations of critically-endangered Whooping Cranes across Vice City. Huge if true. https://t.co/doe6oMOvBX</p><p>— The Birdist (@TheBirdist) <a href="https://x.com/TheBirdist/status/2123441888313090139?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw">November 19, 2026</a></p></blockquote><p><span>In fact, I found a number of avian inaccuracies in just my first few hours of playtime. Certain species like Whooping Crane and Red-cockaded Woodpecker are common throughout the map, despite being endangered and threatened, respectively, in real life. Golden Eagles are extremely rare in Florida, but I found one soaring over the Mt. Kalaga area (mountains, one may point out, aren’t found in Florida either). Some species have confusing naming quirks—the chickadee is Black-capped instead of Carolina, and there’s a “New World Oriole” instead of a Baltimore Oriole—but those changes are likely due to the fact that Carolina and Baltimore don’t exist in the </span><em><span>GTA</span></em><span> universe.</span></p><span data-component-id="nas_patterns:lazyblock-placeholder" data-lazyblock-hash="bean::26796::1795261029">
</span>
<p><br><span>Though some of the inaccuracies may feel like a letdown in a game that is being hailed as the </span><a href="https://www.ign.com/articles/gta-6-is-now-the-joint-highest-rated-game-of-all-time"><span>Greatest of All Time</span></a><span>, I think it’s important to remember that it’s also game where you can </span><a href="https://x.com/Dexerto/status/2123464708082565224"><span>get a ride from a dog driving a jet ski</span></a><span>. </span><em><span>Grand Theft Auto</span></em><span> games are meant to be fun, not science. Plus, despite some of the misfires, the avifauna feels well thought-out. The map is true to the ecological boundaries of South Florida, with herons, gulls, and other waterbirds in the south while birds like Red-bellied Woodpecker, Wood Stork, and American Robin are found farther north. Birders know that certain exotic species have made their home in tropical South Florida, including Spot-breasted Oriole and Red Junglefowl, both in the game.</span></p><div data-component-id="nas_bean:bean-block-green-blockquote">
          <p>  The game doesn’t tell you what species to look for, so you’ll need to cover a lot of ground to find everything. </p>
              
      </div>
<p><span>That geographic fidelity is the main tip for anyone looking to check off all the game’s birds: Find their habitat. The game doesn’t tell you what species to look for, so you’ll need to cover a lot of ground to find everything. Wading birds, shorebirds, and seabirds are common in the southern coastal part of the map. A number of smaller, open-country or forest birds are found up north. Owls come out at night. Birds in zoos and domestic farmyard birds count towards the 60, too, so don’t overlook those areas.</span></p><blockquote><p lang="en" dir="ltr">Wild Boar roadkill. It’s just like birding in Florida! https://t.co/gT84IticOK</p><p>— The Birdist (@TheBirdist) <a href="https://x.com/TheBirdist/status/2123468915695083680?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw">November 19, 2026</a></p></blockquote><div><p><span>The most accurate aspect is the actual landscape. Having birded extensively across South Florida myself, the habitats are immediately recognizable, from the pebbly mangrove beaches at the Keys to the grassy swamps further north. The setting feels absolutely real, from the fonts on the roadside fruit stands to the roadkill alongside the highways. (I found dead wild boar and dead deer by the side of the road, and a Turkey Vulture somehow flew into a building I was in and died at my feet.)&nbsp;</span></p><p><span>So, if you’re up for the kind of funhouse-mirror version of Florida that the game presents, you can have a blast, and adding birdwatching into the insane free-for-all makes for some delightful moments. </span><a href="https://x.com/labradortwister/status/2123372835133689887"><span>This guy just wanted to look at a gull</span></a><span> but ended up chucking a woman off a pier. I’ve made myself laugh out loud by screeching to a halt in the middle of busy highways or intersections and jumping out of my car to look for birds while motorist behind me scream and swear. I love it.<o:p></o:p></span></p></div><!--EndFragment--><blockquote><p lang="en" dir="ltr">easily my favorite thing to do so far is just screech to a halt in the middle of a busy intersection and start birding https://t.co/QsDOLmNfYq</p><p>— The Birdist (@TheBirdist) <a href="https://x.com/TheBirdist/status/2123481848634306841?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw">November 19, 2026</a></p></blockquote><p><span>As I was dodging dirt-biking rednecks to look for species in a northerly forest, I couldn’t help but compare birding in </span><em><span>GTA 6</span></em><span> to </span><a href="https://www.audubon.org/magazine/birding-its-1899-inside-blockbuster-american-west-video-game"><span>birding in </span><em><span>Red Dead Redemption 2</span></em></a><span> (</span><em><span>RDR2</span></em><span>), the latest installment in Rockstar’s other huge franchise. Birds and birding are presented quite similarly in both games, including the binocular “study” mechanic and many of the same species, but the experiences are totally different. </span><em><span>RDR2</span></em><span> was set in the late 1800s, and players rode on horseback or locomotive through a comparatively wide-open and untrammeled version of America. There was mayhem in that game, too—outlaw gangs and marauders—but there were many peaceful moments, too, when a player could look out across wild landscape with nothing but nature as a soundtrack.</span></p><div data-component-id="nas_bean:bean-block-green-blockquote">
          <p> It is, I have to admit, a realistic depiction of birding in South Florida. </p>
              
      </div>
<p><span>Not so in </span><em><span>GTA 6</span></em><span>. There’s plenty of birdsong on the soundtrack, but it competes with an endless racket of car radios, revving engines, swearing pedestrians, and blaring horns. There’s garbage almost everywhere, lapping the shorelines and blowing along the streets. There seems to be no inch of landscape in </span><em><span>GTA 6</span></em><span> that hasn’t been lessened by the touch of humans. It is, I have to admit, a realistic depiction of birding in South Florida.</span></p><blockquote><p lang="en" dir="ltr">Birders working to fill out their GTA VI wildlife checklists should know something I just found out: THERE’S A ZOO https://t.co/2xN0joPKcR</p><p>— The Birdist (@TheBirdist) <a href="https://x.com/TheBirdist/status/2123828346077257919?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw">November 20, 2026</a></p></blockquote><p><span>Though </span><em><span>GTA 6</span></em><span> provides what feels the most accurate version of modern birding ever put to screen, obsessing over the realism misses the point of having fun. Birders can, and should, visit the real South Florida to find the actual birds depicted in the game (and many more), but they can’t do it while also jumping over broken highway bridges on a stolen motorcycle. Birding is a bonus in </span><em><span>GTA 6</span></em><span>; mayhem is the draw.</span></p><p><!--StartFragment--><span>I played for two hours after midnight, then about six straight hours Thursday before having to call it quits to write this column. In that time I found 50 of the 60 species listed in the “birds” compendium. I jumped back in this morning before work and found a huge chunk of species at a zoo (makes sense!) and on a farm, then found the 60th and final, an American Barn Owl, on a water tank in a town in the north of the map. Success!&nbsp;<!--StartFragment--><o:p></o:p><!--EndFragment--><o:p></o:p></span></p><blockquote><p lang="en" dir="ltr">The biggest thing I can confirm is that all 60 birds on this list are available immediately, without needing any story progression. In Red Dead Redemption 2, some bird species were only found in Guarma, only accessible later in the game</p><p>— The Birdist (@TheBirdist) <a href="https://x.com/TheBirdist/status/2123876921976955137?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw">November 20, 2026</a></p></blockquote><!--EndFragment--><p><br>I felt a wave of satisfaction, and while I’m not certain if I was the first one to find them all, when I posted about it, a Rockstar designer replied with, “<a href="https://x.com/AlecMcNamara17/status/2123873474016051679?s=20">History!</a>” I’d met my goal, and celebrated in a way that only <em>Grand Theft Auto</em> games allow: by driving my car full speed off a cliff into the ocean.</p> </section>
                </div></div>]]></description>
        </item>
        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[Perplexity and Intelligence (476 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://openai.com/index/perplexity-and-intelligence/</link>
            <guid>50601525</guid>
            <pubDate>Sun, 22 Nov 2026 17:06:27 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://openai.com/index/perplexity-and-intelligence/">https://openai.com/index/perplexity-and-intelligence/</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50601525">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div><p><span>From afar, it might seem like modern AI is built similarly to how software has been built in previous decades: with a diverse set of techniques targeting specific capabilities. There are indeed a variety of methods, but rather than designing something from scratch, they are mostly </span><i><span>harnessing</span></i><span> the intelligence coming from a much simpler and less interpretable process: training a compressor (or, </span><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shannon%27s_source_coding_theorem" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><span>equivalently</span>⁠<span>(opens in a new window)</span></a><span>, predictor) on a very large corpus of data.</span><sup><span id="citation-top-1"><a href="#citation-bottom-1">1</a></span></sup></p><p><span>I believe tracking the pace of progress on training better compressors is key to understanding the arc of advances in AI. In this post, I argue for establishing perplexity (or, equivalently, cross-entropy loss) as a metric to compare between model developers and inform shared safety standards. As a first step, I provide more transparency into pretraining progress at OpenAI. Publishing our internal perplexity measurements took some consideration; it gives competitors a clearer picture of where we stand, but that concern is outweighed by the urgency of establishing </span><a href="https://darioamodei.com/post/we-must-pace-the-frontier" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><span>shared</span>⁠<span>(opens in a new window)</span></a><span> safety measures across the industry. We at OpenAI hope for this to be a step towards building a shared scientific basis for future pacing efforts.</span></p><p><span>A simplified, but fundamentally informative, view of AI development is that first, one builds a large computer, then uses it to </span><i><span>pretrain </span></i><span>a model (compressor) on a large unstructured dataset, then applies a variety of techniques—like prompting, finetuning or reinforcement learning—in different contexts to elicit the latent capabilities of that model. Algorithmic progress can blur the distinction between training phases, but we can still separate the creation of the AI with hard-to-predict emergent capabilities (which I earlier dubbed “</span><a href="https://openai.com/index/an-alien-mind/"><span>an alien mind</span>⁠</a><span>”) and the subsequent targeted elicitation.</span></p><p><span>The primary way we measure progress on this key “intelligence substrate” has remained remarkably constant over the years—we have been tracking the </span><i><span>perplexity</span></i><span> of the models on a held-out dataset.</span><sup><span id="citation-top-2"><a href="#citation-bottom-2">2</a></span>, <span id="citation-top-3"><a href="#citation-bottom-3">3</a></span></sup><span> Perplexity measures how well a model predicts the next token on average; lower perplexity means better predictions. The plot opening this blog post shows how this metric evolved for our models starting with GPT‑3.5.</span></p><p><span>The range of capabilities of models that are largely described by this one metric is stunning. Setting the new perplexity state-of-the-art on older datasets</span><sup><span id="citation-top-4"><a href="#citation-bottom-4">4</a></span></sup><span>, GPT‑2 was arguably the first model capable of consistently formulating coherent paragraphs of text. The next major leap, GPT‑3, was capable of learning in context, given a few examples of a task. GPT‑3.5, at a log-perplexity of ~0.99 on our current main evaluation dataset, was capable of fluent conversation and formed the basis for the original ChatGPT. GPT‑4, at ~0.91, was proficient at utilizing chain-of-thought reasoning productively, which motivated the subsequent targeted elicitation of this capability and the creation of modern reasoning models. Now, as we have reached losses below 0.8, we have seen the emergence of transformative capabilities in fields like cybersecurity and mathematics. I want to reemphasize that it was not at all clear </span><i><span>a priori</span></i><span> that these capabilities would appear once those precise perplexity thresholds are met—they can fundamentally be seen as emergent latent capabilities from progress on this one metric.</span></p><p><span>As AI advances very rapidly, the scope and way in which we measure downstream capabilities may change quickly. It would make little sense to evaluate GPT‑2 and GPT‑3 on most modern benchmarks; and the benchmarks used then were largely saturated around GPT‑4. In contrast, I believe looking at progress on pretraining is likely to stay informative.</span></p><p><span>A particular change in the pace of progress comes from the increasing contribution of AI to its own development. The most commonly discussed metrics revolve around AI augmenting and replacing human researchers in all stages of the development cycle; with an ultimate inflection point seen at “full recursive self-improvement”, where the process would be entirely automated with no human oversight. Apart from considering such a state as something we should obviously avoid for the sake of maintaining human control, I find this general framing misses something crucial. There is a potential step change that may come from AI participation in one key part of the development process: algorithmic research on pretraining.</span></p><p><span>We have recently witnessed unprecedented acceleration in another domain. For more than a year, we have been posing open problems in mathematics to our models as one of the ways of evaluating them. Once we upgraded to a model of log-perplexity ~0.76, it started producing groundbreaking results. For many of us that have previously worked in mathematics and adjacent fields, seeing the resolutions to questions we’ve long thought about coming out of the model one after another in a single Slack thread felt deeply surreal. Having experienced that allows us to extrapolate—such a sudden transformation is plausible in deep learning research too. While this field relies more on experimentation, there is a long history of simple insights being missed for years; and so as soon as 2027 we could conceivably see a similar avalanche of fundamental pretraining advancements</span><sup><span id="citation-top-5"><a href="#citation-bottom-5">5</a></span></sup><span>. While I expect AI will also significantly contribute to progress on alignment and monitoring, I think that progress is unlikely to be fast enough to allow the full potential acceleration in pretraining.</span></p><p><span>Given that the main way new surprising capabilities emerge is by progress on compression, a measure rooted in perplexity could give us a shared benchmark for general intelligence, regardless of the degree of automation of the development process. Extrapolating from recent progress, we should expect further transformative capabilities as we enter the ~0.68-0.7 log-perplexity window. I don’t know what those capabilities will be, and we may not be testing for them today. At the same time, we should also expect </span><a href="https://alignment.openai.com/cot-monitorability-model-lifecycle" target="_blank" rel="noopener" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade"><span>further degradation</span>⁠<span>(opens in a new window)</span></a><span> in currently dominant forms of alignment auditing and further increases in evaluation and training awareness. I believe frontier model developers should jointly endeavour to establish shared norms for how to evaluate such models and red-team safety cases for them by the time they are first used, including shared transparency standards. I further propose </span><b><span>common limits</span></b><span>, based on perplexity and other measurements, that developers are committed to not crossing until our ability to understand and monitor AI behavior achieves a certain concrete milestone (such as the development of a successor to CoT monitors). That is, I’d like an industry standard that specifies a first limit on development, measured by some key metric we can track across labs, and what concretely we’d need to achieve before going beyond it. I hope that this leads the way to a common framework for setting successive limits and pacing further development.</span></p><p><span>Perplexity as a shared metric has challenges:</span></p><div><ul><li><p><b><span>Difficulty of comparison across labs. </span></b><span>Different developers use different evaluation datasets and train on different data mixtures. Here, we report the numbers on the main dataset we have been tracking; I believe if we establish a goal of transparency we can find transferable ways to compare the quality of different base models.</span></p></li><li><p><b><span>Detachment from actionable evaluations. </span></b><span>Perplexity does not directly correspond to a task we care about. The reason to care about it is that we can expect advances on it to lead to emergence of entirely new capabilities.</span></p></li><li><p><b><span>Not accounting for advances outside of pretraining. </span></b><span>Most notably, chain-of-thought reasoning does not reduce perplexity achieved by the model, even though it leads to a drastic increase in capabilities.</span></p></li><li><p><b><span>Unknown floor. </span></b><span>We do not expect it is possible to achieve a loss of 0, which would correspond to being able to predict the dataset entirely deterministically. But we do not know what the inherent entropy of the data is. As we approach it, the apparent progress on perplexity will slow, but smaller gains on perplexity will correspond to larger leaps in model intelligence.</span></p></li></ul></div><p><span>At the same time, perplexity has stood the test of time as a metric many research teams have put a lot of effort into optimizing, and it continues to deliver consistent gains on general intelligence. I believe it can play an important role in standardizing how we talk about AI progress, and hope to see more transparency about it across the industry.</span></p></div></div>]]></description>
        </item>
