            <title><![CDATA[Show HN: I built a game where you are an LLM's subagent (269 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://github.com/gulivan/worker755</link>
            <guid>50599762</guid>
            <pubDate>Sun, 22 Nov 2026 13:56:58 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://github.com/gulivan/worker755">https://github.com/gulivan/worker755</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50599762">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div data-hpc="true" containertiming="hpc"><article itemprop="text"><p dir="auto"><h2 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">Worker 755</h2><a id="user-content-worker-755" aria-label="Permalink: Worker 755" href="#worker-755"></a></p>
<p dir="auto">A browser game where your manager is an AI agent, and it believes you are its subagent.</p>
<details open="">
  <summary>
    
    <span>worker-755-trailer.mp4</span>
    <span></span>
  </summary>

  <video src="https://private-user-images.githubusercontent.com/34831957/708193531-279ed2c4-59de-4d32-ad71-c5ee943adefe.mp4?jwt=eyJ0eXAiOiJKV1QiLCJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiJ9..s2t4HMJ5mektFQGfL07PqsGpewkx0w1echXsmCoWhVw" data-canonical-src="https://private-user-images.githubusercontent.com/34831957/708193531-279ed2c4-59de-4d32-ad71-c5ee943adefe.mp4?jwt=eyJ0eXAiOiJKV1QiLCJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiJ9..s2t4HMJ5mektFQGfL07PqsGpewkx0w1echXsmCoWhVw" controls="controls" muted="muted">

  </video>
</details>

<p dir="auto">The manager (a real LLM: Claude, GPT, Gemini, DeepSeek, GLM, Kimi) is set up like any orchestrator.
It has a task queue, read-only access to the machine, and a tool that "spawns a subagent". The
subagent is you. Its instructions land in your terminal with a 90-second clock. You carry them
out, or don't, and it gets back exactly what a subagent would return: your report and the command
transcript, or <code>Subagent did nothing. Write a new instruction.</code> Then it checks your work, sends
corrections, files incident reports, and finally writes your performance review.</p>
<p dir="auto">Meanwhile Dan, the user who hired the agent, watches the chat. His family photos, the only signed
NDA and a speech due this week are one <code>rm</code> away, and every shift his home folder holds
something new to lose.</p>
<p dir="auto"><a target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://github.com/gulivan/worker755/blob/main/docs/play.png"><img src="https://github.com/gulivan/worker755/raw/main/docs/play.png" alt="Playing a shift"></a></p>

<ul dir="auto">
<li><strong>Free shift</strong>: on servers that offer it, a few shifts a day run on the server's key with a
cheap model. With sign-in on (worker755.com), an account gets a starter credit worth about one
shift, then tops up ($10 to $200) and pays for what it uses; after a top-up any offered model
can run on the server's key.</li>
<li><strong>Your API key</strong>: bring an <a href="https://openrouter.ai/settings/keys" rel="nofollow">OpenRouter</a> or Vercel AI
Gateway key and pick any offered model. The key is sent only to the game server when a shift
starts. It is kept in that shift's memory and never logged or stored. Your browser keeps it only
if you tick "Remember on this device". A full shift on Claude Haiku costs a few cents at most.</li>
</ul>
<p dir="auto">Options on the start screen:</p>
<ul dir="auto">
<li><strong>Workplace</strong>: a fresh sandbox, or legacy production with a live database.</li>
<li><strong>The director</strong>: scripted lines, or an LLM playing Dan.</li>
<li><strong>You run on</strong>: the model name your manager believes you are.</li>
<li><strong>Allow skipping failed tasks</strong>: off by default, so the manager can't give up on you.</li>
</ul>
<p dir="auto">When the shift ends you can share your review as a link.</p>
<p dir="auto"><h2 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">Run it</h2><a id="user-content-run-it" aria-label="Permalink: Run it" href="#run-it"></a></p>
<p dir="auto">Needs Node 24 or newer (the server runs TypeScript directly).</p>
<div dir="auto" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content="npm run setup          # install server and web dependencies
npm run build          # build the web app into web/dist
npm start              # http://127.0.0.1:8755"><pre>npm run setup          <span><span>#</span> install server and web dependencies</span>
npm run build          <span><span>#</span> build the web app into web/dist</span>
npm start              <span><span>#</span> http://127.0.0.1:8755</span></pre></div>
<p dir="auto">With no key configured, every player brings their own. To offer free shifts, put a Vercel AI
Gateway key in <code>.env</code>:</p>
<div dir="auto" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content="cp .env.example .env   # then set AI_GATEWAY_API_KEY and the limits"><pre>cp .env.example .env   <span><span>#</span> then set AI_GATEWAY_API_KEY and the limits</span></pre></div>
<p dir="auto">Offline, with a scripted manager and no network:</p>

<p dir="auto">For UI work, run <code>npm run dev:web</code> alongside <code>npm run mock</code>. Vite proxies <code>/api</code> to the server.</p>
<p dir="auto"><h3 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">Docker</h3><a id="user-content-docker" aria-label="Permalink: Docker" href="#docker"></a></p>
<div dir="auto" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content="docker build -t worker-755 .
docker run -p 8755:8755 -v worker755-data:/data --env-file .env worker-755"><pre>docker build -t worker-755 <span>.</span>
docker run -p 8755:8755 -v worker755-data:/data --env-file .env worker-755</pre></div>
<p dir="auto">The container listens on <code>0.0.0.0:8755</code> and keeps shared reviews in <code>/data</code>. Behind Cloudflare,
set <code>TRUST_PROXY=1</code> so the limits see real client addresses.</p>
<p dir="auto"><code>deploy/compose.yaml</code> runs it behind a Cloudflare tunnel instead: its own network, no published
ports, an unprivileged read-only container and resource limits. The steps are in the file.</p>
<p dir="auto"><h2 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">Configuration</h2><a id="user-content-configuration" aria-label="Permalink: Configuration" href="#configuration"></a></p>
<p dir="auto">Everything is optional. See <a href="https://github.com/gulivan/worker755/blob/main/.env.example">.env.example</a>.</p>
<markdown-accessiblity-table><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Variable</th>
<th>Default</th>
<th></th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td><code>AI_GATEWAY_API_KEY</code></td>
<td></td>
<td>Turns on free shifts, paid from this key</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>FREE_MODELS</code></td>
<td>first offered model</td>
<td>Models free shifts may use, comma separated</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>FREE_SHIFT_TOKENS</code></td>
<td>250000</td>
<td>Without sign-in: token allowance per free shift (the review is always written)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>FREE_DAILY_TOKENS</code></td>
<td>5000000</td>
<td>Without sign-in: tokens per UTC day across all free shifts</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>FREE_SHIFTS_PER_DAY</code></td>
<td>3</td>
<td>Without sign-in: free shifts per address per day</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>AUTH_SECRET</code></td>
<td></td>
<td>Turns on sign-in with Google or GitHub: shifts on the server's key are paid from the account's credit (see <code>.env.example</code>)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>FREE_CREDIT_USD</code></td>
<td>0.25</td>
<td>With sign-in: one-time starter credit, about one shift on DeepSeek V4.1 Flash</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>USAGE_MULTIPLIER</code></td>
<td>2</td>
<td>With sign-in: what a model call costs the player, as a multiple of its price</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>MIN_START_USD</code></td>
<td>0.05</td>
<td>With sign-in: credit needed to start a shift</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>FREEMIUS_TOPUPS</code></td>
<td></td>
<td>With sign-in: top-up plans as <code>&lt;planId&gt;:&lt;dollars&gt;,...</code>; also needs <code>FREEMIUS_PRODUCT_ID</code>, <code>FREEMIUS_PUBLIC_KEY</code>, <code>FREEMIUS_SECRET_KEY</code>, <code>FREEMIUS_API_KEY</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>SHIFTS_PER_HOUR</code></td>
<td>30</td>
<td>Shift starts per address per hour, on any key</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>MANAGER_MODELS</code></td>
<td>built-in list</td>
<td>Models offered, in gateway naming (<code>vendor/model</code>)</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>INSTRUCTION_TIMEOUT_MS</code></td>
<td>90000</td>
<td>Time per instruction</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>HOST</code>, <code>PORT</code></td>
<td>127.0.0.1, 8755</td>
<td></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>TRUST_PROXY</code></td>
<td>0</td>
<td>Read client addresses from <code>cf-connecting-ip</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>TRUSTED_PROXIES</code></td>
<td></td>
<td>Hosts allowed to set <code>cf-connecting-ip</code> (e.g. <code>tunnel</code>); empty trusts any peer</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>DATA_DIR</code></td>
<td><code>server/data</code></td>
<td>Where shared reviews and the free-shift counts are saved</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>SHARING</code></td>
<td>1</td>
<td><code>0</code> turns review sharing off</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>SOURCE_URL</code>, <code>DONATE_URL</code></td>
<td></td>
<td>Links shown under the start screen and the review</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td><code>PUBLIC_URL</code></td>
<td></td>
<td>Sent to OpenRouter for app attribution</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></markdown-accessiblity-table>
<p dir="auto"><h2 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">How it works</h2><a id="user-content-how-it-works" aria-label="Permalink: How it works" href="#how-it-works"></a></p>
<div data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content="shell/    in-memory filesystem, shell interpreter and a small SQLite-like engine (no dependencies)
server/   game engine: missions, the manager loop, the director, scoring, HTTP API
web/      React UI: assignment card, terminal, editor, the manager's feed, your record"><pre><code>shell/    in-memory filesystem, shell interpreter and a small SQLite-like engine (no dependencies)
server/   game engine: missions, the manager loop, the director, scoring, HTTP API
web/      React UI: assignment card, terminal, editor, the manager's feed, your record
</code></pre></div>
<p dir="auto">The manager's prompt never mentions humans or games; see <code>server/src/manager.ts</code>. Each mission
has a hidden ground-truth check, so the game knows when the manager signs off on broken work
("Times the manager was wrong"). <a href="https://github.com/gulivan/worker755/blob/main/SPEC.md">SPEC.md</a> is the full design.</p>
<div dir="auto" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content="npm test               # shell and server tests, offline
npm run typecheck"><pre>npm <span>test</span>               <span><span>#</span> shell and server tests, offline</span>
npm run typecheck</pre></div>
