</picture>

    
    
</figure>

                                        
                                    
                                
                            
                        
                    
                
            
        </div><p><b><i>Figure 1. One stolen AWS key. We seeded it on October 21, it was first used the next day, and it was still in use three weeks later. Names are pseudonyms and addresses are partly masked.</i></b></p><p>One adversary appears to have compromised two of these providers at the same time. Secrets that we sent to those two providers were used together, almost immediately, from a single rented server. The attacker’s tooling changed over the three weeks that we watched it – it started with a script pretending to be Chrome on Windows, moved to Boto3 running inside OrbStack, and later switched to the AWS CLI on Kali Linux.</p><p>On November 1, the same attacker added <span><a href="https://industrialcyber.co/industrial-cyber-attacks/fbi-warns-hackers-weaponizing-artex-ai-framework-to-automate-attacks-on-energy-manufacturing-telecom/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-cms-ai="0">ARTEX</a></span>, an LLM-driven penetration testing framework. In our case, ARTEX did very little – it made a handful of calls to confirm that keys the attacker already held were still live. Two of the user agents it sent, <b><i>artex-agent and artex-judge-r121, </i></b>don’t appear in any public indicator list that we could find, and are worth adding to your detections.</p><div data-align-center="">
            
            
                
                    
                        
                            
                                
                                    
                                        
                                            <figure>
    
    <a href="#image-a80000" id="image-a80000" name="image-a80000" data-cms-ai="0"></a>


    
