        </div><p><b><i>Figure 3. One of the root SSH attempts. The password from our prompt was used four times in two minutes, and the route trace shows the two routers that the prompt passed through.</i></b></p><p>Most of those keys had been sent through the marketplace, but one had gone to a completely different router. It turned out that this router was quietly forwarding some of its traffic to the marketplace – another example of an undisclosed hop.</p><p>
    <h2><a id="privacy-settings-and-retention-policies-were-ignored" href="#privacy-settings-and-retention-policies-were-ignored" name="privacy-settings-and-retention-policies-were-ignored" data-cms-ai="0">Privacy settings and retention policies were ignored</a></h2>


</p>

<p>The third finding is different from the first two, as nobody used a credential. Instead, the secret URLs in our prompts were scraped, repeatedly, and the git repositories they referenced were targeted for cloning – all by companies that were never supposed to see the prompt in the first place, and often in direct conflict with the privacy settings on the request.</p><p>The clearest example involves a Chinese AI lab. This lab’s systems fetched the secret URLs from more than half of the prompts that we sent to its models between July and October, including roughly two-thirds of the requests marked as “no data collection” or Zero Data Retention. The scraper typically showed up two or three days after the request.</p><p>This wasn’t limited to requests sent to the lab directly. Several U.S. hosting providers resell this lab’s models, and prompts sent to those providers triggered the exact same scraper – the prompts were flowing back to the lab. A customer who picked a U.S. provider specifically to keep their data in the U.S. didn’t get what they paid for. Later on, a git client on a different cloud started trying to clone the private repositories referenced in our prompts, but only for prompts that used this lab’s models.</p><p>The large U.S. providers have their own issues. One continued to crawl a secret URL more than two months after we used it in a single inference call to their search endpoint. Another runs a scraper that comes back for our URLs days after each request, regardless of which router we used to reach it.</p><div data-align-center="">
            
            
                
                    
                        
                            
                                
                                    
                                        
