        </div><p><b><i>Figure 4. One day of incidents, grouped by source network. The same scraper networks show up against prompts that we sent through several different routers.</i></b></p><p>The strangest incident involved a company chat. Twenty-four days after we sent a prompt to one Chinese provider, the secret link from that prompt was posted into a Feishu chat channel (Feishu is known as Lark outside of China). Within two minutes, the link was opened from three desktops and three phones in Beijing and Shanghai. Our disclosure to this provider only included the first eight characters of that link, so whoever shared it was reading from a stored copy of the full prompt, more than three weeks after the request was sent to a U.S.-based provider with the ZDR flag.<br></p><div data-align-center="">
            
            
                
                    
                        
                            
                                
                                    
                                        
