    <h2><a id="how-do-canary-tokens-detect-prompt-leaks" href="#how-do-canary-tokens-detect-prompt-leaks" name="how-do-canary-tokens-detect-prompt-leaks" data-cms-ai="0">How do canary tokens detect prompt leaks?</a></h2>


</p>

<p>A canary token is a secret that only exists in one place – if anyone ever uses it, you know that place has been exposed. We started in July by seeding prompts with <span><a href="https://canarytokens.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-cms-ai="0">Thinkst Canarytokens</a></span>, and every request carried an AWS key, a DNS name, and a URL that had never been seen anywhere else. Tokens started firing within days, and the tokens from July are still firing today.</p><p>The proof-of-concept worked so well that it made sense to build a dedicated service just for seeding LLM prompts. This service generates a unique seed, and uses it to derive unique hostnames, URLs, and credentials for every prompt and then runs the services that those credentials point to – DNS, web, git, SSH, databases, mail, and a long tail of older protocols. The AWS keys are real keys tied to empty accounts with full logging. Every request that touches a token is tied back to the exact prompt, model, router, and privacy setting that carried it. Since each unique component can be tied back to its parent seed, we’re able to get precise timing and sequencing; we know what DNS server they use, how long it took to reply, and every piece of the inbound connections (TCP, TLS SNI, HTTP Host headers, URLs, credentials etc.). </p><div data-align-center="">
            
            
                
                    
                        
                            
                                
                                    
                                        
