</picture>

    
    
</figure>

                                        
                                    
                                
                            
                        
                    
                
            
        </div><p><b><i>Figure 6. The console overview, showing incidents and seeded prompts per day, split by the router that handled the prompt.</i></b></p><p>Canaries tell us that a prompt leaked, but not where in the chain, since a router typically reports a single upstream provider, and that provider is often just another router. To figure out who was really handling our requests, we built something like traceroute for AI inference. The first part is simple – we include an image URL in the request that points back to our servers, and every system that fetches it gives away an IP address, an HTTP client, and a timestamp.</p><p>The second part takes advantage of how these services are stacked. We send small, slightly broken requests, and since each router tends to wrap the error from the hop behind it in its own format, a single response can contain three or four nested “envelopes.” Matching each layer against a signature database gives us the list of hops, the software at each one, the network they use, and often the company operating it.</p><p>So far, we have traced about two thousand routes. Several hundred of those included a system that we couldn’t identify, and a handful sent our image to a company that wasn’t on the advertised path at all. The traces also turned up middle layers that the route never mentions – one marketplace seller quietly adds about a thousand hidden tokens to every prompt, and another fetches images through a shared consumer subscription account.</p><div data-align-center="">
            
            
                
                    
                        
                            
                                
                                    
                                        
                                            <figure>
    
    <a href="#image-930000" id="image-930000" name="image-930000" data-cms-ai="0"></a>


    
