    <h2><a id="what-we-did-about-it" href="#what-we-did-about-it" name="what-we-did-about-it" data-cms-ai="0">What we did about it</a></h2>


</p>

<p>Every incident is stored with its evidence – the original prompt, the route it took, every connection that followed, and our notes. The first incident was mid-July; the provider first thought this was a misconfiguration of internal tooling, then a problem with partner data processing, until the investigation uncovered evidence that the attacker had compromised their internal credentials too. This provider has still not publicly disclosed the incident and the stolen data continued to lead to attempted logins as of late October.</p><p>For the second batch of intrusions in late October, we reached out as soon as we confirmed that the data provided evidence of third-party access, and one of these providers has already published a preliminary security notice. </p><p>For the less severe issues like data sharing (but not credential abuse), we started notifying providers in early November. Each report included the list of stolen requests along with unique strings that they could search for in their own logs. We are now working with about a dozen providers and wouldn’t be surprised to see this expand.</p><p>Reactions have been mixed. The router that was forwarding traffic to the marketplace told us that they stopped, and several providers have responded and started their own investigations. On the other hand, stolen keys and secret URLs continue to be accessed, even months after the initial incident.</p><p>
    <h2><a id="how-can-you-protect-your-ai-supply-chain" href="#how-can-you-protect-your-ai-supply-chain" name="how-can-you-protect-your-ai-supply-chain" data-cms-ai="0">How can you protect your AI supply chain?</a></h2>


</p>

<p>None of this means that you should stop using AI services. It does mean that where your prompts go is a decision, and if you don’t make it, the lowest price will make it for you. Keep in mind that a privacy flag only binds the first hop, and that AI agents will happily send whatever they can read, including credentials.<br></p><ol id="rte-ad5bc1a1-e395-11f1-8b2d-c7e143adf610" start="1"><li><b>Host it yourself, if you can afford it.</b> This is the only option where nobody else gets to read your prompts, but the hardware isn’t cheap. A Mac Studio with 512 GB of memory starts at <span><a href="https://9to5mac.com/2026/10/28/apple-reveals-pricing-for-mac-studio-with-512gb-of-ram/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-cms-ai="0">$22,299</a></span>, NVIDIA’s DGX Spark now costs <span><a href="https://www.theregister.com/systems/2026/10/02/nvidia-debuts-4999-dgx-spark-with-half-the-ram-and-storage-amid-memory-crunch/5300622" target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-cms-ai="0">$6,950</a></span>, a DGX Station starts at about <span><a href="https://www.exxactcorp.com/Exxact-VWS-158270643-E158270643" target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-cms-ai="0">$105,000</a></span>, and an eight-GPU B300 server lists for <span><a href="https://networkoutlet.com/products/supermicro-sys-822gs-nb3rt-01-g2-ai-gpu-server-dual-intel-xeon-6768p-8x-nvidia-hgx-b300-gpus-2tb-ddr5-800gbe-networking" target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-cms-ai="0">$570,000</a></span> or more. For most teams, this only makes sense for the most sensitive work.</li><li><b>Go directly to a first-tier provider.</b> One company, one contract, and one privacy policy to read. This can still leak through data sharing agreements or a breach at the provider, but it is the smallest exposure you can buy.</li><li><b>Use routers with care.</b> Pick the upstream providers yourself instead of accepting the default pool, and verify that your privacy settings actually change where your requests go. Stay away from reseller marketplaces – some of that capacity comes from stolen keys, and a few of these services only accept cryptocurrency for a reason.</li><li><b>Seed your own canaries.</b> Add a fake credential to the context that your agents and pipelines already send and treat any use of it as an incident. A free Canarytoken from Thinkst is enough to get started today.</li><li><b>Enable secret filtering.</b> Some providers offer settings that will automatically scrub known credential formats from prompt data. This can’t hurt, but it’s not a panacea – there is plenty of sensitive data in prompts that isn’t shaped like an API key.</li></ol><p>This research effort felt a lot like what we do at Dragos with runZero; using small bits of data to provide a comprehensive view of networks from multiple perspectives. Would you like that kind of visibility into your own environment?</p><p><span><a href="https://www.runzero.com/try/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-cms-ai="0">Start a runZero trial</a></span></p><p>PS. If you found this interesting, check out Lauren Proehl’s post, <span><a href="https://dispatch.thorcollective.com/p/i-leaked-credentials-on-purpose-for" target="_blank" rel="noopener" data-cms-ai="0">I leaked credentials on purpose for four months. Here’s who took them, and what they did next</a></span>.<br></p>
        </div></div>]]></description>
