</tr>
<tr>
<td>C and C++ (gcc, clang or Intel oneAPI icx/icpx: <code>clang -O2 main.c lib.c</code>)</td>
<td>the same C bridge, through LoadLibrary or dlopen</td>
<td>✅</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>C# / .NET 8+ (<code>dotnet run -c Release</code>)</td>
<td>the same C bridge, through NativeLibrary; profiled with EventPipe, nothing to install</td>
<td>✅</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></markdown-accessiblity-table>
<p dir="auto">It ports top-level, deterministic functions that take plain data (numbers, strings, lists, dicts, typed arrays…), including ones in your entry script when its main code sits under <code>if __name__ == "__main__":</code> (or <code>if __FILE__ == $0</code>, or <code>if (import.meta.main)</code> / <code>if (require.main === module)</code>). In Python that means module-level functions and <code>@staticmethod</code>s, in Java, C# and PHP static methods (and PHP functions), in Go plain functions, in C and C++ free functions and static methods. <code>scan</code> says why anything else can't move yet.</p>
<p dir="auto">With Intel oneAPI on Windows, run fast-anything from a shell where <code>setvars.bat</code> ran (it sets up the linker). SYCL kernels run on the device and aren't ported; with <code>-fsycl</code>, only host functions are profiled and ported.</p>
<p dir="auto">Ruby, Java, Go, PHP, C, C++ and C# need no headers, bindings or extra tools: the port is a plain Rust library with one C entry point taking and returning JSON, and fast-anything replays the recorded calls against it directly. Any language that can call C can use that bridge.</p>
<p dir="auto"><h2 tabindex="-1" dir="auto">License</h2><a id="user-content-license" aria-label="Permalink: License" href="#license"></a></p>
<p dir="auto">MIT</p>
</article></div></div>]]></description>
