            <title><![CDATA[Toyota, Nissan, and Honda to Standardize Parts to Lower Costs (1189 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://www.caranddriver.com/news/a74490092/toyota-nissan-honda-standardize-parts-lower-costs/</link>
            <guid>50596133</guid>
            <pubDate>Sun, 22 Nov 2026 04:04:56 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://www.caranddriver.com/news/a74490092/toyota-nissan-honda-standardize-parts-lower-costs/">https://www.caranddriver.com/news/a74490092/toyota-nissan-honda-standardize-parts-lower-costs/</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50596133">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div data-journey-body="standard-article"><ul data-node-id="0"><li aria-level="1"><a href="https://caranddriver.com/toyota" target="_self" data-vars-ga-outbound-link="https://caranddriver.com/toyota" data-vars-ga-ux-element="Hyperlink" data-vars-ga-call-to-action="Toyota"><strong>Toyota</strong></a><strong>, </strong><a href="https://caranddriver.com/nissan" target="_self" data-vars-ga-outbound-link="https://caranddriver.com/nissan" data-vars-ga-ux-element="Hyperlink" data-vars-ga-call-to-action="Nissan"><strong>Nissan</strong></a><strong>, and </strong><a href="https://caranddriver.com/honda" target="_self" data-vars-ga-outbound-link="https://caranddriver.com/honda" data-vars-ga-ux-element="Hyperlink" data-vars-ga-call-to-action="Honda"><strong>Honda</strong></a><strong> will begin standardizing some vehicle parts in an effort to lower production costs, according to a report by </strong><a rel="noopener" href="https://www.autonews.com/toyota/an-japan-industrywide-auto-parts-standardization-1119/" target="_blank" data-vars-ga-outbound-link="https://www.autonews.com/toyota/an-japan-industrywide-auto-parts-standardization-1119/" data-vars-ga-ux-element="Hyperlink" data-vars-ga-call-to-action="Automotive News"><em><strong>Automotive News</strong></em></a><strong>. </strong></li><li aria-level="1"><strong>The automakers will start with equalized quality criteria for steel, consistent protocols for plastics, and a universal color-coding scheme for wiring harnesses.</strong></li><li aria-level="1"><strong>Discussions are starting with Japan’s domestic suppliers, though international suppliers will be looped in soon.</strong></li></ul><p data-journey-content="true" data-node-id="1">Japan’s three largest automakers have agreed to begin standardizing vehicle parts in an effort to reduce costs, according to <em>Automotive News</em>. The outlet reports that Japan Automobile Manufacturers Association chairman Koji Sato, who is also the vice chairman of Toyota, announced the decision at a news conference in Tokyo this week.</p><p data-journey-content="true" data-node-id="3">In the short term, the standardization efforts will begin with unified quality criteria for steel, consistent protocols for plastics and resins, and a universal color-coding scheme for wiring harnesses. In the long term, the Japan Automobile Manufacturers Association has plans to move to more components that cover the entire car. The three areas were chosen as the first to be standardized because they are parts that are generally not noticed by consumers, and they shouldn’t have an impact on any brand’s individual identity. All three are also areas that account for significant production costs for the automakers, meaning they can have an immediate impact.</p><p><span data-node-id="5"><div size="large" data-embed="body-image" data-lazy-id="P0-17" id="c8edcad2-9d63-4fb5-9d10-aa0f29523dbd"><p><img draggable="true" alt="An Auto Parts Manufacturer Company in Binzhou" title="An Auto Parts Manufacturer Company in Binzhou" loading="lazy" width="5472" height="3648" decoding="async" data-nimg="1" sizes="100vw" srcset="https://hips.hearstapps.com/hmg-prod/images/9a437d5d-fb1e-4298-b80d-345012dac005.jpeg?crop=1xw:0.844xh;0xw,0.105xh&amp;resize=768:* 640w, https://hips.hearstapps.com/hmg-prod/images/9a437d5d-fb1e-4298-b80d-345012dac005.jpeg?crop=1xw:0.844xh;0xw,0.105xh&amp;resize=768:* 980w, https://hips.hearstapps.com/hmg-prod/images/9a437d5d-fb1e-4298-b80d-345012dac005.jpeg?crop=1xw:0.844xh;0xw,0.105xh&amp;resize=980:* 1120w, https://hips.hearstapps.com/hmg-prod/images/9a437d5d-fb1e-4298-b80d-345012dac005.jpeg?crop=1xw:0.844xh;0xw,0.105xh&amp;resize=980:* 1400w, https://hips.hearstapps.com/hmg-prod/images/9a437d5d-fb1e-4298-b80d-345012dac005.jpeg?crop=1xw:0.844xh;0xw,0.105xh&amp;resize=980:* 1800w, https://hips.hearstapps.com/hmg-prod/images/9a437d5d-fb1e-4298-b80d-345012dac005.jpeg?crop=1xw:0.844xh;0xw,0.105xh&amp;resize=980:* 2000w" src="https://hips.hearstapps.com/hmg-prod/images/9a437d5d-fb1e-4298-b80d-345012dac005.jpeg?crop=1xw:0.844xh;0xw,0.105xh&amp;resize=980:*"></p><p><figcaption data-theme-key="photo-credit-figcaption"><span data-theme-key="photo-credit-creditor">CFOTO/Future Publishing via Getty Images</span><span>|</span><span>Getty Images</span></figcaption></p></div></span></p><p data-journey-content="true" data-node-id="6">The first discussions of standardization are set to include Japan’s domestic automotive parts suppliers, though international suppliers will be brought into the fold in time. The first standardization efforts are set to go into place next year, according to the <em>AN</em> report.</p><p data-journey-content="true" data-node-id="7">The push to simplify and lower costs comes as Japan’s seven automakers, including Toyota, Nissan, Honda, Mazda, Subaru, Mitsubishi, and Suzuki, work to fend off the increasingly competitive list of Chinese automakers. The automakers are said to hope that they can channel the money saved by homogenizing into new innovations.</p><p data-journey-content="true" data-node-id="8">“We all share this strong sense of crisis, creating a momentum for change,” Sato said. “Based upon our sense of a crisis, we decided to choose these three areas.”</p><hr><h2 id="skip-the-lot-let-car-and-driver-help-you-find-your-next-car"><strong>➡️ Skip the lot. Let Car and Driver help you find your next car.</strong></h2><div data-journey-blur="partial" data-ad-exclude="true" data-fp-ignore="true"><p><span><img src="https://hips.hearstapps.com/rover/profile_photos/fac2a745-e58e-49fc-be50-42f30a2ea607_1775311013.file?fill=1:1&amp;resize=120:*" alt="Headshot of Jack Fitzgerald" title="Headshot of Jack Fitzgerald" width="100%" height="100%" decoding="async" loading="lazy"></span></p><div><p><span>Jack Fitzgerald’s love for cars stems from his as yet unshakable addiction to Formula 1. After a brief stint as a detailer for a local dealership group in college, he knew he needed a more permanent way to drive all the new cars he couldn’t afford and decided to pursue a career in auto writing. By hounding his college professors at the University of Wisconsin-Milwaukee, he was able to travel Wisconsin seeking out stories in the auto world before landing his dream job at </span><i><em>Car and Driver</em></i><span>. His new goal is to delay the inevitable demise of his 2010 Volkswagen Golf.</span></p></div></div></div></div>]]></description>
        </item>
